{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Change 1727 got LLM-Review +1 with 5 advisories. I read them before CI finished rather than\nwaiting, because on 1717 the advisories caught two real bugs both times. Same again: one of\nthese was a hole straight through the floor this change exists to be.\n\n1. (correctness) THE IMPORTANT ONE. `run_sweep` stamped the last-sweep clock in its `finally`\n   — including when it had just STOOD ASIDE because the store lock was busy. A stand-aside\n   folds nothing, so stamping it resets the staleness arm for a full interval. Under sustained\n   contention every trigger would stand aside, stamp, and go quiet: the store would never\n   compact while LOOKING freshly swept. That is worse than having no floor, because the signal\n   says it is working.\n\n   What makes this worth dwelling on: I introduced it while fixing a different review finding.\n   Stamping in a `finally` reads as tidy bookkeeping, and the guard I had just added (stand\n   aside when busy) is exactly what turned tidy bookkeeping into a silent failure mode. Fixed\n   by deleting the stamp from `run_sweep` entirely — `compact_all_cli` already stamps when it\n   actually sweeps, and that is the only event the stamp may record. Which also resolved\n   advisory 4 (the stamp was being written twice per successful sweep). Added a test that a\n   stand-aside leaves the clock alone AND that the next trigger therefore still fires.\n\n2. (iac) `| tee` under `set -o pipefail` made the captured `rc` the pipeline's rightmost\n   non-zero exit, so a `tee` failure (unwritable RUNNER_TEMP, full disk) would masquerade as\n   rebar's exit code — defeating the exit-2-vs-1 branching I had just added tests for two\n   rounds ago. Replaced the pipe with a redirect plus `cat`.\n\n3. (tests) The dry-run stamp guard was untested. Added: a dry run leaves the clock unset, a\n   real sweep sets it.\n\n4. (maintainability) Double `record_sweep` — resolved by fix 1.\n\n5. (maintainability) My own explanatory comment in `close_ticket` still said \"Compaction is NOT\n   run here\" and described the trigger as \"an out-of-band sweep that runs in a DEDICATED\n   CLONE\". Both stale: the close DOES trigger compaction now (it just delegates), and I had\n   already corrected the dedicated-clone claim in the docs and the workflow while missing this\n   third copy. Rewritten to say what is actually true — what changed is who holds the lock and\n   when, not whether a close leads to compaction.\n\nGates green after all five: ruff, mypy 526 files, complexity ratchet unchanged, module-size\ncontract. Both tiers re-running before I re-push."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786620782445810001,"uuid":"f63d8d1b-1da2-40fc-853d-0d2abc0f92ef"}